<Doc
  title="Concepts"
  description="The vocabulary mcpd uses: traces and spans, span kinds, root and event spans, the six signals, correlation, and the model hierarchy from generation down to chunk."
  lede="A handful of words carry the whole data model. The rest of the documentation assumes you know them, so this page defines each one and says what it is for."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Span</h2>
  <p>
    A span is one unit of work. It has a name, a kind, a start time, usually an
    end time, and a status. It can carry what went in, what came out, how many
    tokens it used, what it cost, and an error if it failed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep the name low-cardinality. <code>search_docs</code> is a name;
    <code>search_docs(query=hello)</code> is a name and a value glued together,
    and it makes grouping by name useless the moment you have two runs.
  </p>

  <h2>Trace</h2>
  <p>
    A trace is every span from one run, joined by a 128-bit trace id. Inside the
    trace, <code>parent_span_id</code> gives the tree its shape: a tool call made
    inside a model step is a child of that step, so the nesting on screen is the
    nesting that actually happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ids are OpenTelemetry-shaped, 32 lowercase hex characters for a trace and 16
    for a span. That is not decoration. It is what lets a trace that started in
    your web tier and ended in your agent stay one trace across both, and what
    lets mcpd sit beside whatever else already reads your telemetry.
  </p>
  <p>
    A span whose parent came from a foreign tracing system carries it in
    <code>external_parent_span_id</code> instead. mcpd keeps that value for
    correlation but never builds its tree from it, because a parent id that
    names a span mcpd never received would hang the whole subtree under nothing.
  </p>

  <Waterfall class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    mcpd also stores a projection of each trace: the span count, the error
    count, total tokens, total cost, how many model calls and how many tool
    calls. That row is recomputed from the spans on every write rather than
    incremented, because spans arrive out of order, get retried and get updated
    in place. A counter drifts under all three, and a span count that is visibly
    wrong in the list makes a reader distrust every other number on the page.
  </p>

  <h2>Root spans and event spans</h2>
  <p>
    A root span is one with no parent inside the trace. It is normally an
    <code>agent_run</code> or a <code>workflow_run</code>, and it is where the
    trace gets its name, its tags and its correlation. Tags are only meaningful
    on a root, where they label the whole run.
  </p>
  <p>
    An event span is a point in time rather than a duration, so it has no end
    and no duration. A streamed chunk is the usual case. The distinction is
    stored rather than inferred from a missing end time, because "this never
    had an end" and "this has not finished yet" are different facts and only
    one of them is a problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    A trace is complete once its root has ended and no span is still open. Until
    then it is still listed, with the best name available, rather than hidden
    until it finishes. The run that never finished is often the one you came to
    look at.
  </p>

  <h2>Span kind</h2>
  <p>
    The kind says what an operation <em>is</em>. It decides how the span is drawn,
    which attributes mean anything on it, and whether cost accounting looks at
    it. mcpd defines 33 kinds, in families:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Family</th><th>Kinds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Agent</td>
        <td><code>agent_run</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Model</td>
        <td>
          <code>model_generation</code>, <code>model_step</code>,
          <code>model_inference</code>, <code>model_chunk</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tools</td>
        <td>
          <code>tool_call</code>, <code>client_tool_call</code>,
          <code>provider_tool_call</code>, <code>mcp_tool_call</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Handoffs</td>
        <td>
          <code>handoff</code>, when one agent passes control to another
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Workflow</td>
        <td>
          <code>workflow_run</code>, <code>workflow_step</code>,
          <code>workflow_conditional</code>, <code>workflow_parallel</code>,
          <code>workflow_loop</code>, and the rest
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Retrieval</td>
        <td>
          <code>rag_ingestion</code>, <code>rag_embedding</code>,
          <code>rag_vector_operation</code>, <code>rag_action</code>,
          <code>retrieval</code>, <code>memory_operation</code>,
          <code>graph_action</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Evaluation</td>
        <td>
          <code>scorer_run</code>, <code>scorer_step</code>,
          <code>guardrail</code>, <code>processor_run</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Other</td>
        <td>
          <code>mapping</code>, <code>skill_resolution</code>,
          <code>workspace_action</code>, <code>http_request</code>,
          <code>generic</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Four kinds bill tokens: <code>model_generation</code>,
    <code>model_step</code>, <code>model_inference</code> and
    <code>rag_embedding</code>. Those are the spans cost accounting reads.
    <code>generic</code> is always valid and never inferred, so a span of an
    unrecognised kind is labelled <code>generic</code> rather than guessed at.
    <a href="/docs/reference/data-model" class="link">The data model</a> lists
    every kind with what it means.
  </p>

  <h2>The model hierarchy</h2>
  <p>
    Four of those kinds nest inside each other, and the nesting is the most
    useful thing in the model:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>model_generation</code> is one logical generation: everything from
      the first request to the final answer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>model_step</code> is one step inside it. A generation that calls
      tools twice has three steps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>model_inference</code> is the provider round-trip alone, with no
      processors and no tool execution inside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>model_chunk</code> is one streamed chunk, recorded as an event.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The reason for four levels rather than one is that "the model call was slow"
    is almost never true as stated. One call from your code is not one HTTP
    round trip once tools are involved: it is a request, a tool, another
    request, a guardrail, another request. Collapse that into a single span and
    a run that spent six seconds in your own retry loop looks identical to one
    that spent six seconds waiting on a provider. Separating inference from step
    from generation is what makes the difference readable, and the first chunk
    event is what gives you time to first token.
  </p>
  <p>
    Record token usage <strong>once</strong>, on the span that made the provider
    call. The trace projection sums input and output tokens across every span in
    the trace, so the same counts attached to a generation and again to each of
    its steps will double the token total and double the cost on screen. The
    hierarchy is for timing; the tokens belong to whichever level actually spoke
    to the provider.
  </p>

  <h2>The six signals</h2>
  <p>
    Spans are one of six things mcpd stores. Logs are the lines a run emitted.
    Metrics are numeric observations, as counters, gauges or histograms. Scores
    are a machine's evaluation of a span or a trace. Feedback is a person's. An
    aspect note is one sentence about a run, written after it finished. A model
    writes most of them. The daemon writes the <code>outcome</code> notes, from
    the run's status and other facts it already holds.
  </p>

  <SignalFlow class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    All six carry the trace id they belong to, most of them the span id as well,
    and all six pass through the same redaction pipeline before anything is
    written. A secret leaks out of a log line exactly as well as out of a
    prompt.
  </p>
  <p>
    A score always declares its scale: the value, and the minimum and maximum it
    sits between. Every SDK requires both at the call site, because a bare 0.7
    with no scale attached cannot be compared with anything, including the same
    scorer's output last week.
  </p>
  <p>
    Feedback is kept in a separate place from scores, and never averaged with
    them. It comes in five shapes: <code>thumbs</code>, <code>rating</code>,
    <code>correction</code>, <code>tag</code> and <code>comment</code>. A
    correction is the most valuable of the five, because it is the only signal
    that carries the right answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Correlation</h2>
  <p>
    Correlation is the context a signal belongs to. It is the difference between
    a pile of runs and a set you can slice.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it holds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>project</code></td><td>The tenancy key. Every signal belongs to exactly one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>environment</code></td><td>Where it ran, such as production or staging.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>service_name</code></td><td>The emitting service.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>service_version</code></td><td>Its version, so a regression can be pinned to a release.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>session_id</code></td><td>A user-facing session. Groups many traces into one conversation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>thread_id</code></td><td>A thread within that session.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>user_id</code></td><td>The end user the run was for.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>resource_id</code></td><td>A tenant or workspace inside your own application.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>run_id</code></td><td>Your own identifier for this run.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>request_id</code></td><td>The inbound request that caused the work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>experiment_id</code></td><td>Set when an experiment produced the run.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>release</code></td><td>A release name or a git sha.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>project</code> is the only required field. When a sender does not name
    one, the daemon files the signal under its <code>--project</code> default, so
    nothing is ever stored with nowhere to belong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Set <code>session_id</code> even when it looks redundant. One agent run is a
    trace, but a complaint is almost always about a conversation, and without a
    session id there is nothing that turns eleven separate traces back into the
    exchange the person actually had.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trace list filters on <code>environment</code>,
    <code>session_id</code>, <code>thread_id</code> and <code>user_id</code>
    directly, alongside status, kind, name, tags, model, time range, minimum
    duration, minimum cost and score.
    <a href="/docs/searching" class="link">Finding a run</a> puts them together.
  </p>

  <h2>Links</h2>
  <p>
    A span has one parent and any number of links. A link points at another span
    or another trace and says why: <code>follows_from</code>,
    <code>retry_of</code>, <code>replay_of</code>, or whatever you name it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Links exist because parentage cannot express fan-in. A batch job caused by
    twenty requests has twenty links and one parent. Forcing that into the tree
    would either invent nineteen duplicate subtrees or throw nineteen of the
    causes away.
  </p>

